<section id="framework">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">The Framework</div>
    <h2>Three Structural Failures Behind Activation Drop-Off</h2>

    <div class="purple-squirrel-box">
      <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--gray); line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        A drop-off in your activation funnel is a structural failure in the system's
        onboarding architecture — not a copy problem, not a UI problem, and not a user deficiency.
        I audit the architecture to find the layer your analytics cannot instrument.
      </p>

      <div class="venn-diagram">
        <div class="venn-item">
          <div class="venn-title">Churn Signals</div>
          <div class="venn-desc">
            Your support tickets and NPS verbatims describe frustration — not the architecture
            behind it. Users can't tell you the information sequence was wrong. They tell you
            they felt lost. The audit translates that signal into a structural finding: the exact
            point where the system's assumptions exceeded the user's current state.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="venn-item">
          <div class="venn-title">Onboarding Architecture</div>
          <div class="venn-desc">
            Your onboarding flow was sequenced around how your product works, not around
            how users construct a mental model of it under uncertainty. Cognitive load,
            information sequencing, and decision points must be governed by the user's
            current state — not by the product's internal logic. That requires the same
            rigor as your database schema.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="venn-item">
          <div class="venn-title">Activation Economics</div>
          <div class="venn-desc">
            Trial-to-paid conversion is flat despite UI improvements and copy iterations
            because those changes operate at the surface layer. Activation failures are
            one level deeper — in the decision architecture. Each percentage point of
            improved activation compounds directly into LTV. The audit finds where that
            point is being lost.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="intersection-note">
        Your analytics team has been instrumenting the right events at the wrong layer.
        The drop-off is visible in the data. The cause — the structural mismatch between
        what your system assumes about the user and what the user actually knows at that
        moment — is not visible in any event log. That is the layer this audit addresses.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="adr-quote">
      Live production evidence: the same architectural discipline applied to a language-learning
      platform serving real users since 2011. The friction event that surfaced in 2024 became
      the proof of method.<br/>
